\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Europanto (ISO 639-3 eur, deprecated) is a humorous macaronic ``language'' created in 1996 by Diego Marani, a translator for the Council of the European Union. It mixes European vocabulary with loose, speaker-chosen grammar. According to Wikipedia, Marani was reportedly no longer actively promoting it as of 2005. It has no native speakers, no fixed grammar and no standard orthography, so a comprehensive K-12 curriculum conducted in Europanto is not a meaningful goal. This dossier records only what was found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found. No textbooks or school programmes exist in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Europanto: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europanto}
\item Worldwide Words, article on Europanto: \url{https://worldwidewords.org/tp-eur2.html}
\item Mediamatic, Diego Marani on Europanto (1999): \url{https://www.mediamatic.net/en/page/8718/europanto}
\item Rosetta Project, entry for the code: \url{https://rosettapanglossia.longnow.org/index.php?title=Eur}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found beyond the pages above. Wikipedia mentions Marani's newspaper columns (e.g. in \emph{Le Soir Illustr\'e}) and a novel written in Europanto; the search did not give a link to any of these texts.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Europanto could at most serve as a classroom example of language play, code-mixing or European multilingualism, within a language-awareness unit. It is not suited to being a language of instruction.
\end{document}
